0.008087158203125, 0.0134124755859375, -0.00820159912109375, -0.02032470703125, 0.0133514404296875, 0.031890869140625, -0.0171661376953125, -0.01849365234375, -0.0011043548583984375, -0.01383209228515625, -0.0306396484375, -0.01739501953125, -0.0309600830078125, -0.003997802734375, -0.016876220703125, 0.0160675048828125, -0.0253753662109375, 0.0305023193359375, -0.0013628005981445312, -0.053375244140625, -0.016265869140625, -0.0225372314453125, 0.00501251220703125, 0.00914764404296875, 0.00495147705078125, -0.006458282470703125, -0.00589752197265625, -0.0124359130859375, 0.0080413818359375, 0.016204833984375, -0.0079498291015625, -0.00372314453125, 0.004314422607421875, 0.00409698486328125, 0.01497650146484375, 0.0268707275390625, -0.0246429443359375, 0.0207672119140625, 0.0210723876953125, -0.0203094482421875, -0.01555633544921875, -0.004299163818359375, -0.0017271041870117188, 0.03948974609375, 0.01094818115234375, 0.005859375, 0.03253173828125, 0.0018186569213867188, 0.007228851318359375, 0.04315185546875, 0.0194854736328125, 0.0035152435302734375, -0.03277587890625, -0.012237548828125, -0.007434844970703125, -0.002719879150390625, -0.042572021484375, -0.0014467239379882812, 0.019775390625, -0.00226593017578125, -0.006732940673828125, -0.03057861328125, -0.010223388671875, 0.026123046875, 0.0284423828125, -0.031768798828125, 0.037200927734375, 0.00589752197265625, -0.00159454345703125, 0.0020732879638671875, -0.0117950439453125, -0.0141754150390625, 0.010223388671875, -0.01800537109375, 0.0183563232421875, -0.01143646240234375, 0.01477813720703125, 0.0035419464111328125, 0.025299072265625, -0.0070953369140625, 0.023956298828125, -0.0020542144775390625, -0.0293426513671875, 0.057098388671875, -0.020477294921875, -0.048828125, -0.00942230224609375, 0.027984619140625, -0.0361328125, -0.00377655029296875, 0.01253509521484375, 0.0546875, -0.0064544677734375, 0.0247650146484375, -0.008575439453125, 0.019683837890625, 0.008697509765625, 0.003917694091796875, -0.01245880126953125, -0.02056884765625, -0.0007476806640625, 0.0223388671875, -0.0228271484375, -0.0157623291015625, 0.033660888671875, 0.004291534423828125, 0.020172119140625, -0.00046372413635253906, -0.0089569091796875, -1.7404556274414062e-05, -0.0022983551025390625, -0.035919189453125, -0.01381683349609375, 0.0029926300048828125, 0.01739501953125, 0.0225067138671875, -0.01204681396484375, 0.021484375, 0.0004410743713378906, -0.0170745849609375, 0.015960693359375, 0.0081939697265625, -0.004520416259765625, 0.0089111328125, 0.0097808837890625, 0.03436279296875, -0.018463134765625, -0.00424957275390625, -0.0022411346435546875, 0.023284912109375, -0.013031005859375, -0.0026340484619140625, 0.0247802734375, -0.02435302734375, -0.000888824462890625, -0.01294708251953125, -0.0081634521484375, 0.0180511474609375, -0.0023097991943359375, 0.0253448486328125, -0.04949951171875, 0.01143646240234375, 9.08970832824707e-05, 0.005695343017578125, -0.004528045654296875, 0.015777587890625, -0.01079559326171875, 0.01033782958984375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Classical mean-field theory for large random spiking networks often asserts that macroscopic network behavior is determined solely by aggregate statistics\u2014specifically, the *weak convergence* of the associated input measures for each synaptic type\u2014as the fast-synapse limit is approached. In this framework, only the global rates or distributions of excitation and inhibition are considered relevant, and the fine timing of individual spikes is presumed to \u201caverage out.\u201d\n\nTo precisely state and analyze this claim, we first introduce all pertinent notation and terminology and clarify foundational concepts.\n\n---\n\n**1. Notation, Terminology, and Model Specification**\n\n- **$N$**: Number of neurons in the network.\n- **$q_N$**: Out-degree, i.e., the number of outgoing synapses per neuron. We have $q_N\\to\\infty$ and $q_N/N\\to0$ as $N\\to\\infty$, corresponding to sparse but increasingly well-sampled networks.\n- **$\\lambda_N$**: Alternate notation often used for mean degree (particularly in Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi random graphs); in our setting, $q_N$ plays this role, with $\\lambda_N = q_N$ unless otherwise specified.\n- **$K(t)$**: Synaptic kernel giving the time course of the postsynaptic current generated by a single spike. In the *fast-synapse* limit, its support and amplitude shrink as $N$ grows (maintaining constant area).\n- **$a, b$**: Amplitudes (strengths) of excitatory and inhibitory synaptic pulses, respectively.\n- **$x$**: Membrane potential just prior to any input.\n- **$\\theta$**: Voltage threshold for spike emission.\n- **$\\tau$**: Temporal delay between two incoming pulses.\n- **$\\pi_N, \\pi$**: Empirical and limiting proportions of neuron types (e.g., excitatory/inhibitory).\n- **$O_P(\\cdot)$, $o_{L^1}(1)$**: Probability and mean notations for asymptotics.\n\nThe network is modeled as a block-structured random directed graph, satisfying Dale's principle: each neuron is strictly excitatory or inhibitory, and connection patterns adhere to these types. Neuron dynamics follow the standard *threshold-reset* rule: a neuron spikes when its membrane potential reaches $\\theta$, then resets.\n\n**2. Definitions: Weak and Componentwise Weak Convergence**\n\n- **Weak convergence**: A sequence of synaptic input measures $\\mu_N$ (which can be thought of as convolved spike trains) converges *weakly* to a measure $\\mu$ if, for any continuous test function $f$, $\\int f(t)\\,d\\mu_N(t)\\rightarrow \\int f(t)\\,d\\mu(t)$ as $N\\to\\infty$. Importantly, weak convergence ensures agreement in aggregate but says nothing about the ordering or precise timing of individual spikes.\n- **Componentwise weak convergence**: When the network has multiple synaptic input types (e.g., excitation, inhibition), weak convergence is assessed independently for each type\u2019s input sequence.\n\nThese concepts underpin mean-field theory\u2019s claim that only the limiting aggregate quantities\u2014synaptic rates or input measures\u2014matter for population-scale dynamics; microscopic spike timing details are presumed irrelevant.\n\n**3. Main Result: Failure of Mean-Field Equivalence Under Fast, Sparse Synapses**\n\nContrary to classical intuition, we show that in threshold-reset spiking neurons with *sparse connectivity* and *rapidly decaying (delta-like) synaptic kernels*, componentwise weak convergence does **not** guarantee equivalence of population-level firing statistics. Even when the net excitation and inhibition are matched in the weak limit, the precise *order* in which excitatory and inhibitory spikes arrive can systematically bias whether an individual neuron fires. This order sensitivity is missed by standard mean-field approaches.\n\n**4. Illustrative Example: Excitatory-First vs. Inhibitory-First Input**\n\nConsider a single neuron with initial membrane potential $x$ (just prior to any input) and threshold $\\theta$. Two input protocols are engineered so that their aggregate input measures converge (componentwise) to the same limit as input pulses become infinitesimally narrow and closely spaced ($\\tau\\downarrow 0$):\n\n| Protocol              | $t = -\\tau$   | $t = 0$         | Post-Input Voltage | Spikes?   |\n|-----------------------|---------------|-----------------|--------------------|-----------|\n| **Excitatory-first**  | $+a$ (E)      | $+b$ (I)        | $x+a$ after E; $x+a-b$ after I | Spikes if $x+a \\geq \\theta$|\n| **Inhibitory-first**  | $+b$ (I)      | $+a$ (E)        | $x-b$ after I; $x+a-b$ after E| Spikes if $x+a-b \\geq \\theta$|\n\n- In the *excitatory-first* case, if $x+a\\geq \\theta$ but $x+a-b<\\theta$, the excitation alone pushes the potential over threshold, causing a spike and reset before inhibition arrives.\n- In the *inhibitory-first* case, both inputs sum subthreshold ($x+a-b < \\theta$), and no spike occurs.\n\nBecause the aggregate input measures are identical in the weak limit, mean-field theory cannot distinguish these cases. Yet, for initial conditions with $x+a-b < \\theta \\leq x+a$, the neuron\u2019s spiking is determined solely by input *order*. \n\n**Intuitive Summary of Population-Level Difference:**  \nIn a random population of neurons with continuously distributed initial potentials, approximately half of those whose voltages fall within the \u201cthreshold window\u201d ($x+a-b < \\theta \\leq x+a$) will fire in the excitatory-first protocol but not in the inhibitory-first. Since this configuration arises with nonzero probability and persists as $N\\to\\infty$, the expected difference in firing proportion converges in mean to $1/2 + o_{L^1}(1)$. This factor $1/2$ arises because, for uniformly random potentials in the relevant interval, exactly half land in the order-sensitive range.\n\n**5. Extension to Networks and Robustness to Topology**\n\nApplying either input protocol to all neurons in a sparse, block-structured network, we observe\u2014by the above logic\u2014a persistent population-level difference in firing count that remains as $N\\to\\infty$. The effect generalizes to other network topologies, including sparse Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi graphs (with $q_N$ or $\\lambda_N$ diverging but much less than $N$), and is robust to the tracking of *probe neurons*: i.e., individual neurons followed over extended time, possibly across multiple threshold resets. \u201cProbe neurons\u201d are not special except that they are monitored individually; their firing records empirically exhibit the same order-sensitivity. Furthermore, the phenomenon persists in more complex networks, including those with bounded but nonvanishing out-degree, provided the timing and structure of pulses match the \u201cfast synapse\u201d regime.\n\n**6. Exceptions, Stable Regimes, and Asymptotic Statement**\n\nThis macroscopic order-sensitivity requires specific conditions: sparse connectivity and synaptic kernels with negligible temporal width (delta-like pulses). In *stable* regimes\u2014where all synaptic kernels have strictly positive minimal delay and only finitely many distinct types and delays\u2014the order effect vanishes except for a set of initial conditions and parameters of Lebesgue measure zero (i.e., only isolated, nongeneric configurations allow persistent order-sensitivity; such cases are vanishingly rare in practice).  \nAnalytically, on sparse, typewise-mixing graphs (e.g., directed Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi with edge probability $q_N/N$), prior to any threshold crossing, the population dynamics converge (with high probability) to a delayed mean-field system, with deviations from mean-field behavior bounded in probability by $O_P(q_N^{-1/2} + \\|\\pi_N - \\pi\\|_1)$. Thus, away from the fast-synapse, sparse limit, mean-field theory retains its classical validity almost everywhere.\n\n---\n\n**7. Summary Table: Protocols and Spiking Outcomes**\n\n| Initial Voltage $x$     | Excitatory-First Outcome    | Inhibitory-First Outcome | Population Difference   |\n|------------------------|----------------------------|-------------------------|------------------------|\n| $x+a-b < \\theta \\leq x+a$ | Spike                     | No spike                | Yes (contributes $1/2$ per neuron in expectation) |\n| else                   | Same outcome for both      | Same outcome            | None                  |\n\n---\n\n**References and Proof Outline**\n\nFor further details, combinatorial arguments, and rigorous proofs, see Appendix A. Briefly: under random initial conditions with continuous density, the probability a neuron is within the \u201cwindow\u201d is proportional to $b$, and symmetry implies a $1/2$ expectation for order-sensitivity among those neurons.\n\n---\n\nThis analysis demonstrates that in certain fast, sparse regimes, weak convergence and even componentwise weak convergence are insufficient to uniquely determine network behavior\u2014a counterintuitive failure of the mean-field paradigm rooted in the persistence of microscopic input order."}}